title: LSP Configuration

!! Where the LSP server comes from

The VS Code extension starts the wiki's LSP server itself, as a child of the window, and stops it when the window closes. It picks the wiki in this order:

# The `tiddlywiki.lsp.port` setting, when set: the extension connects to that port and starts nothing
# A workspace folder whose `tiddlywiki.info` has an `lsp` section with `"autostart": true`
# The `tiddlywiki.lsp.wiki` setting
# A wiki already running with `--lsp`, found through its `.tw-mcp/lsp` file

The second and third run a command from the workspace, so they only happen in a trusted workspace. When nothing is found, the log says how to name a wiki, and a wiki started later with `--lsp` is still picked up.

!!! The lsp section of tiddlywiki.info

```
"lsp": {
	"autostart": true,
	"label": "lsp-mcp-server",
	"command": "node ../scripts/tw.js --core dev"
}
```

|! Key |! Default |! Meaning |
|`autostart` |off |`true` lets the editor start this wiki |
|`label` |`lsp-<wiki folder>` |Name of the server in both logs and in the status bar. `--lsp label=<name>` on the command line wins |
|`command` |`tiddlywiki` |Boots the wiki. The wiki folder, `--lsp pipe=<name>` and `label=<name>` are appended; relative paths start from the wiki folder |

Put the section in the edition you run, usually the `-server` one. Only the `tiddlywiki.info` of the wiki you start counts: an included wiki's `lsp` section is ignored. When two workspace folders both have one, the extension prefers the folder that includes the other.

!!! Settings, for a wiki that is not yours to change

The `tiddlywiki.info` of a TiddlyWiki checkout belongs to that repository. The same values can live in the VS Code settings instead, for example in a `.code-workspace` file:

```
"settings": {
	"tiddlywiki.lsp.wiki": "${workspaceFolder}/editions/tw5.com-server",
	"tiddlywiki.lsp.command": "tiddlywiki +plugins/wikilabs/tw-mcp-core +plugins/wikilabs/tw-mcp",
	"tiddlywiki.lsp.label": "lsp-tw5.com-server"
}
```

`tiddlywiki.lsp.wiki` takes an absolute path, `${workspaceFolder}/...`, `${workspaceFolder:<name>}/...` in a multi-root workspace, or a path relative to the first workspace folder. The command names the plugins because that edition's `tiddlywiki.info` does not list them.

!!! A fallback for every workspace

Set in your user settings, the same values apply to every workspace without an `lsp` section or settings of its own. The answers then come from that wiki: core macros and shadow tiddlers resolve, but links to the workspace's own tiddlers are marked missing.

!!! Seeing which server is active

The status bar names the LSP server by its label, and the MCP server it is linked to, as in `lsp-mcp-server ⇄ sse-primary`. A spinner means the wiki is still booting, a warning that it is not running. The tooltip gives the wiki folder and where it was configured; a click opens the `TiddlyWiki LSP` log.

Try it in [[LSP and MCP - (Example)]].